{"name":"Cold Canada","hex":"#dbfffe","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a low temperature.","Causing the air to be cold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A condition of low temperature.","A harsh place; a place of abandonment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cold"},{"term":"canada","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A traditional Portuguese unit of liquid volume equal to 1.7–2.1 liters depending on the area of Portugal, used particularly for wine."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of cañada (“a ravine, a gully”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canada"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cold","blocks":[{"text":"Cold is the presence of low temperature, especially in the atmosphere. In common usage, cold is often a subjective perception. A lower bound to temperature is absolute zero, defined as 0.00 K on the Kelvin scale, an absolute thermodynamic temperature scale. This corresponds to −273.15 °C on the Celsius scale, −459.67 °F on the Fahrenheit scale, and 0.00 °R on the Rankine scale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg/330px-Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold"},{"title":"Canada","blocks":[{"text":"Canada is a country in North America. Its ten provinces and three territories extend from the Atlantic Ocean to the Pacific Ocean and northward into the Arctic Ocean, making it the second-largest country by total area, with the longest coastline of any country. Its border with the United States is the longest international land border. The country is characterized by a wide range of both meteorologic and geological regions. With a population of over 41 million, it has widely varying population densities, with the majority residing in its urban areas and large areas being sparsely populated. Its capital is Ottawa and its three largest metropolitan areas are Toronto, Montreal, and Vancouver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Flag_of_Canada_%28Pantone%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Canada_%28Pantone%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canada"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px-Diving_signal_cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px-Diving_signal_cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Signal \"cold\" – unofficial (except recommended by CMAS), it is nonetheless used by many schools of diving and propagated through diving websites as one of the more useful additional signals[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiving_signal_cold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Goose_bumps.jpg/500px-Goose_bumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Goose_bumps.jpg/500px-Goose_bumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Goose bumps, a common physiological response to cold, aiming to reduce the loss of body heat in a cold environment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoose_bumps.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg/500px-AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg/500px-AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A photograph